/**
\fn pdg::Sprite::setupAnimationPhysics(AnimationPhysicsDefinition definition)
\brief Create a physical rig for the current pose.

Validates the definition before creating bodies and joints. Creates an ordinary root PhysicsBody if needed, temporarily makes it kinematic, and suspends its collision filter. Clip/controller evaluation supplies the desired pose; physical bodies then supply the displayed result.

Each configured bone has a Part named exactly after the bone. Obtain it with
findPart() and operate on its PhysicsBody through the read-only `.physics`
property. The Part's local transform is the capsule offset from the bone; its
world transform follows the solved body. Positions and application points use
owning-layer coordinates, and velocities use distance units or radians per second.

A pre-existing Part with that name is reused only if it is bound directly to the
same bone, has the same capsule offset and unit unreflected scale, has no parent
Part, and has neither a PhysicsBody nor a Collider. Conflicts reject the configuration without replacing
caller-owned Parts. While enabled, body mass, inertia, materials, velocities,
impulses, forces, torque, damping and drive targets are available through `.physics`.
The definition fixes bone bindings and capsule offsets: disable the
physical rig before changing those or removing a mapped Part/body. Disable keeps
the Parts and their names/IDs but returns their `.physics` properties to NoPhysics.
Independently retained bodies keep their last state and detach to the basic solver.

Capsules and pivot/rotary-limit joints are shared Collider and PhysicsConstraint
components, available through each Part's collider and physics properties.
The Sprite's physics.getMass() reports attached component mass; setMass() scales
those components proportionally. Part mass edits and explicit attach/detach
operations update the total without redistributing other masses. Whole-Sprite
impulses target the selected physical root. Contact notifications use the shared
ColliderContact event; enable them with collider.setWantsContactEvents(true).
Initial Sprite/Layer snapshots preserve the active rig and its control state.

\param definition Physical rig object described below.

\return No value.

\note Call enableAnimationPose() successfully before using this method. The Sprite must belong to a physics-enabled SpriteLayer, and supportsAnimationPhysics() must be true. The example assumes a bone named shoulder.

\par Example
\code{.js}
sprite.setupAnimationPhysics({
    bodies: [{ bone: 'shoulder', mass: 1, length: 10, radius: 1 }],
    rootMode: pdg.animationRoot_Fixed
});
const shoulder = sprite.findPart('shoulder');
const body = shoulder.physics;
const state = body.getState();
body.applyImpulse(new pdg.Vector(2, 0), new pdg.Point(state.x, state.y));
body.applyTorque(0.5, 0.25); // torque integrated over a quarter second
\endcode

\htmlonly[block]
<div class="shared-api-options">
\endhtmlonly

\anchor animation_physics_definition
\par Physical animation definition
| Top-level field | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `version` | 1 | Definition format; only integer version 1 is supported. |
| `bodies` | Required | Nonempty array of body objects (at most 65,536), described below. Array indices identify bodies. |
| `joints` | [] | Array of joint objects (at most 65,536), described below. |
| `rootMode` | animationRoot_Fixed | Fixed keeps the Sprite frame independent; Follow publishes the selected body's translation to the Sprite. Both support mixed body modes. |
| `rootBody` | 0 | Body index used for root follow and release velocity. |
| `selfCollisions` | false | Whether physical animation bodies may collide with one another. |

| Body field | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `bone` | Required | Bone name or ID in the current rig; one body per driven bone. |
| `mass`, `length`, `radius` | Required | Finite mass > 0, capsule length >= 0, and radius > 0 in rig distance units. |
| `mode` | animationBody_Dynamic | Dynamic simulation or animationBody_Kinematic following the desired pose. |
| `offsetX`, `offsetY`, `offsetRotation` | 0 | Bone-local placement; rotation is in radians. |
| `friction` | 0.7 | Finite nonnegative contact friction. |
| `elasticity` | 0 | Finite bounce coefficient from 0 to 1. |
| `categories`, `mask` | 4294967295 | Unsigned 32-bit collision category and mask bits. |

| Joint field | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `parent`, `child` | Required | Body indices connected by the joint. |
| `parentX`, `parentY`, `childX`, `childY` | 0 | Joint anchor positions in the respective body frames. |
| `minAngle`, `maxAngle` | -Math.PI, Math.PI | Finite relative angular limits in radians. |
| `maxForce` | 1000000 | Finite positive joint force limit. |
| `collide` | false | Whether the connected bodies may collide. |

Fixed-root mode keeps the Sprite frame authoritative. Follow-root mode publishes
the selected body's translation while preserving the Sprite's rotation and scale.
The selected body can be Kinematic, Dynamic or Driven. Sprite movement and rotation
edit the animation target frame: controlled root bodies sweep or drive toward it,
and dynamic descendants react through their joints. Dynamic bodies are not
teleported. Pose/Part queries between an edit and the next solve do not discard
the target, and followed translation does not feed back into a drive target.
Use scheduled movement/rotation or an animation helper for a continuous path.

The Sprite and driven bones require unit effective scale magnitudes; reflection is
supported. `setFlipX()`/`setFlipY()` also reflect an active rig about the Sprite's
local axes through its origin. Positions, linear/angular velocities, collider
frames, internal anchors, rotary limits and rig recovery state follow the flip.
Body, collider, shape and constraint identities and mass/inertia are preserved.
Detached parts remain independent. Bone/Part queries, artwork and mounts use the
same reflected frames. World-space manual drives, IK targets, forces and torques
retain their world targets; gravity is unchanged.

External linear joints retain their outside anchors. Disconnect external angular
joints before flipping; an unsupported boundary joint, a locked solver, or a pose
callback rejects the flip without changing it. Arbitrary live scaling does not
resize physical bodies. Create the rig outside callbacks and a locked physics
step; existing Sprite joints and a second enabled rig are rejected.

\htmlonly[block]
</div>
\endhtmlonly

\see disableAnimationPhysics(), findPart(), PhysicsBody::getState(), supportsAnimationPhysics()
*/
